{"records":[{"id":"m6vrdbwsnc","title":"Raw, uncropped supporting Western blot files for: Lignitto L. et al. ... Pagano M.,  Nrf2 activation promotes lung cancer metastasis by inhibiting the degradation of Bach1. Cell 2019","description":"Raw, not cropped supporting Western blot files for: Lignitto L., LeBoeuf S., Homer H., Jiang S., Askenazi M., Karakousi T., Pass H., Bhutkar A., Tsirigos A., Ueberheide B., Sayin V., Papagiannakopoulos T, and Pagano M.  Nrf2 activation promotes lung cancer metastasis by inhibiting the degradation of Bach1.  Cell 2019.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-06-27T18:52:48.001Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/m6vrdbwsnc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/m6vrdbwsnc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Michele","last_name":"Pagano","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Luca","last_name":"Lignitto","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Cell Biology","Cancer","Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7pk2dcp6zp","title":"Influence of  pharmacological AMPK activation on Nrf2- hyperactive lung cancer cells (A549 cell line)","description":"Data originate from assessment of viability, migration, gene expression, knockdown and overexpression approaches as well as determination of ROS, NAD+ and NADH levels in metformin-treated A549 cells (including optimization experiments).   They are connected with the study starting with the hypothesis (based on previous data) that AMPK activation by metformin would  lead to phosphorylation and degradation of NRF2 in the KEAP-deficient background of A549 lung cancer cells and blunt the hyperactive protective shield of NRF2. \nChecking NRF2 half -life, regulated target genes and sensitvity to taxol and cisplatin in A549 cells showed no influence of metformin. But metformin reduced migration and BACH1 levels of A549 cells. Testing for an AMPK /BACH1/motility axis showed that metformin blunts migration in an AMPK and BACH1 independent manner.  Metformin leads to reduced relative NAD+ levels which is causally involved in reduced migration as supplementation with NMN can restore motility in metformin treated cells.  Most data is published open access in doi: 10.1038/s41598-026-61444-3.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-09-24T23:07:28.645Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7pk2dcp6zp","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7pk2dcp6zp"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Shara","last_name":"Sosa Cabrera","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Elke","last_name":"Heiss","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/013tf3c58","name":"FWF Austrian Science Fund","grant_id":"P33778"}],"categories":["Cell Biology","Biochemical Pharmacology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xm87k23hrh","title":"Kudo et al, Cancer Cell 2020","description":"Protein Kinase λ/ι induces Autophagy, Oxidative phosphorylation, and NRF2 to promote liver cancer progression.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-06-21T00:53:32.109Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xm87k23hrh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xm87k23hrh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Moscat","last_name":"Diaz-Meco","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yotaro","last_name":"Kudo","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Liver Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vffmxyn7cy","title":"Original western blots","description":"The Original western blots of “Sappanchalcone Suppresses Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer Cells by Oxidative Stress-Driven DNA Damage and ER Stress Activation through PIEZO1 Modulation”","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-17T14:46:07.044Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vffmxyn7cy","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vffmxyn7cy"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"WeiYu","last_name":"Wu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ngttmkkhkn","title":"ALDOA-mediated metabolic reprogramming is a targetable vulnerability for ferroptosis sensitization in cancer","description":"Ferroptosis presents great potential for cancer therapy, either alone or in combination with classical therapy. However, inducing ferroptosis by targeting canonical ferroptosis suppressors that directly inhibit lipid peroxidation non-selectively induces ferroptosis in both cancerous and normal cells, thereby limiting its therapeutic potential. In this study, we reveal that aldolase A (ALDOA) reprograms lipid metabolism to resist ferroptosis in cancer cells and identify ALDOA as a targetable vulnerability for ferroptosis sensitization. Cancer cells with ALDOA suppression exhibit increased susceptibility to ferroptosis-a response less obvious in normal cells. Mechanistically, ALDOA depletion induces significant accumulation of fructose 1,6-bisphosphate in cancer cells, thereby enhancing autophagy-dependent degradation of phospholipid-modifying enzymes. These alterations increase the ratio of phospholipids containing pro-ferroptotic polyunsaturated fatty acids over anti-ferroptotic monounsaturated fatty acids, culminating in heightened ferroptosis sensitivity. Moreover, ALDOA inhibitors selectively promote ferroptosis in cancer cells, both in vitro and in vivo. Collectively, our findings reveal that ALDOA-mediated metabolic reprogramming is a targetable vulnerability for ferroptosis sensitization in cancer.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-27T01:37:28.511Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ngttmkkhkn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ngttmkkhkn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"pengqi","last_name":"wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"sheng","last_name":"ding","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":null,"name":"Tsinghua-Peking Center for Life Sciences","grant_id":null},{"id":null,"name":"Beijing Natural Science Foundation","grant_id":"JQ22016"},{"id":null,"name":"New Cornerstone Investigator Program","grant_id":null},{"id":null,"name":"National Key R&D Program of China ","grant_id":"2022YFA1103704"}],"categories":["Lipidomics","Metabolomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7x6j47zxx5","title":"Unprocessed microscopy and immunoblotting images for data presented in Ahsan et al. Molecular Cell Volume 86 Issue 13 (2026), \"Reductive death is averted by a conserved de novo lipogenic switch\"","description":"Biguanides, including metformin, the world’s most prescribed oral hypoglycemic, extend health span and lifespan in vertebrates and invertebrates. Given the widespread use and apparent safety of metformin, it is assumed that its effects are not associated with toxicity, except when in marked excess. Here we determine that accumulation of damaging reducing equivalents is an unanticipated toxicity associated with biguanides, the defense against which requires post-transcriptional protection of de novo fatty acid biosynthesis. We demonstrate that biguanide treatment during impaired fatty acid biosynthesis drives NADPH toxicity, leading to catastrophic elevation of NADH/GSH reducing equivalents and accelerated death across metazoans. Multiple NADPH-generating interventions require fatty acid biosynthesis to prevent markedly shortened survival, indicating that this defense mechanism is broadly leveraged. We propose that fatty acid biosynthesis is a tunable rheostat which can minimize biguanide-induced reductive stress whilst maximizing its pro-longevity outcomes and serve as an exploitable vulnerability in reductive stress sensitive cancers. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-07-08T17:21:25.51Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7x6j47zxx5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7x6j47zxx5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Fasih","last_name":"Ahsan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alexander","last_name":"Soukas","orcid":"0000-0002-9100-2436"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Redox Metabolism","Diabetes","Caenorhabditis elegans","Aging","Cell Growth","Translation (Protein Synthesis)","Fatty Acid Synthase","Metformin"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"28gvnkd8r4","title":"Raw and processed GC-MS lipidomics and LC-MS metabolomics data presented in Ahsan, et al. Molecular Cell Volume 86 Issue 13 (2026), \"Reductive death is averted by a conserved de novo lipogenic switch\" ","description":"Biguanides, including metformin, the world’s most prescribed oral hypoglycemic, extend health span and lifespan in vertebrates and invertebrates. Given the widespread use and apparent safety of metformin, it is assumed that its effects are not associated with toxicity, except when in marked excess. Here we determine that accumulation of damaging reducing equivalents is an unanticipated toxicity associated with biguanides, the defense against which requires post-transcriptional protection of de novo fatty acid biosynthesis. We demonstrate that biguanide treatment during impaired fatty acid biosynthesis drives NADPH toxicity, leading to catastrophic elevation of NADH/GSH reducing equivalents and accelerated death across metazoans. Multiple NADPH-generating interventions require fatty acid biosynthesis to prevent markedly shortened survival, indicating that this defense mechanism is broadly leveraged. We propose that fatty acid biosynthesis is a tunable rheostat which can minimize biguanide-induced reductive stress whilst maximizing its pro-longevity outcomes and serve as an exploitable vulnerability in reductive stress sensitive cancers. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-07-08T17:21:20.327Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/28gvnkd8r4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/28gvnkd8r4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Fasih","last_name":"Ahsan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alexander","last_name":"Soukas","orcid":"0000-0002-9100-2436"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Redox Metabolism","Diabetes","Caenorhabditis elegans","Aging","Cell Growth","Translation (Protein Synthesis)","Fatty Acid Synthase","Metformin"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"b232zdtg3m","title":"Isoorientin data","description":"Research Data of the  manuscript \"Isoorientin reverses lung cancer drug resistance by promoting ferroptosis via the SIRT6/Nrf2/GPX4 signaling pathway\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-02-13T14:57:00.662Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/b232zdtg3m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/b232zdtg3m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"senling","last_name":"feng","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Reference Material","Database"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4wwcjg93gh","title":"LKB1inactivation elicits an NNMT-mediated methyl sink and confers dependence on PRMT5 in lung cancer","description":"The protein arginine methyl transferase 5 (PRMT5) emerges as a therapeutic target in S-methyl-5'-thioadenosine phosphorylase (MTAP)-deleted cancers, where MTA accumulation partially inhibits its  activity. However, It remains unclear whether other genetic alterations can dictate PRMT5 activity in cancer. Here we identify LKB1 as an alternative predictor of PRMT5 inhibition in lung cancer independent of MTAP. Mechanistically, LKB1 loss activates SIK1/2-CRTC2 signaling to upregulate NNMT, creating a \"methyl sink\" that lowers the SAM/SAH ratio and attenuates PRMT5 activity. NNMT overexpression is sufficient to induce this hypomorphic PRMT5 state and heighten sensitivity to PRMT5 inhibitors. Functionally, PRMT5 inhibition induces senescence in LKB1-deficient cells and confers vulnerability to navitoclax, synergistically blunting tumor growth in vivo. Collectively, we identify PRMT5 as an actionable therapeutic vulnerability in LKB1-deficient lung cancer, and propose LKB1 status/NNMT expression as potential biomarkers for PRMT5 inhibition. These findings may expand the clinical utility of PRMT5-targeted therapies beyond MTAP-deleted cancers. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-05-01T11:15:27.93Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4wwcjg93gh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4wwcjg93gh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"WEN","last_name":"MI","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zsbwnb2b6m","title":"Sulforaphane activates Nrf2 to attenuate diabetic bladder dysfunction in a time-dependent manner","description":"Diabetic bladder dysfunction (DBD) is a common and severe complication of diabetes mellitus, and there is a lack of effective interventions to slow its progression. This study aimed to investigate the critical role of the endogenous antioxidant transcription factor nuclear factor erythroid 2-related factor 2 (Nrf2) pathway in the DBD, specifically within a type 1 diabetes mellitus (T1DM) model, and to validate whether its natural agonist, sulforaphane (SFN), exerts protective effects against the DBD by activating the Nrf2 pathway. Using a streptozotocin (STZ)-induced T1DM mouse model alongside Nrf2 knockout (Nrf2-KO) mice, this study systematically investigated the therapeutic role of SFN and its Nrf2-dependent mechanisms during both the compensatory and decompensatory stages of the DBD. In wild-type T1DM mice, SFN treatment significantly improved voiding function and suppressed bladder oxidative stress, inflammation, fibrosis, and apoptosis. However, all the protective effects of SFN were abolished in Nrf2-KO T1DM mice. Mechanistic studies demonstrated that SFN specifically activated Nrf2 and its downstream target genes, HO-1 and NQO1, in bladder tissue. This study confirms that Nrf2 activation plays a central role in delaying DBD progression. SFN protects the bladder protection by directly activating the Nrf2 signaling pathway and enhancing downstream antioxidant defenses, providing a new theoretical basis and potential therapeutic strategy for DBD management.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-12-05T01:07:57.979Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zsbwnb2b6m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zsbwnb2b6m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Hewei","last_name":"Xu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Functional Urology"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":120325,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":134,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":120325,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":119781,"restricted":61,"embargoed":483},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2057},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":602},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":442},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":401},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":398},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":391},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":356},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":305},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":243},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":209}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":117748},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1899},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":175},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":120},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":73},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":65},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":52},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":35},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":28},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":488},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":400},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":350},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":321},{"id":"48c8fa0f-965c-5cbf-a859-a5363432478b","name":"Universidade de Sao Paulo","count":288}]}}